        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Great Zimbabwe</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1000 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Made by</span><span class="info-stat-val">Ancestral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shona">Shona</a> people, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karanga">Karanga</a>, descendants of the Gumanye, who settled the site from around 1000; the stone walls were raised by its ruling dynasty over more than 300 years</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Form</span><span class="info-stat-val">Three groups of mortarless granite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dry-stone-construction">dry-stone</a> walls, the Hill Complex, the Great Enclosure and the Valley Complex, spread over 7.22 square kilometres, the Great Enclosure's walls up to 11 metres high and about 250 metres long around a conical tower</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where it is now</span><span class="info-stat-val">In the south-eastern hills of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a> near Masvingo, a national monument and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>; most of the soapstone birds are back in Zimbabwe, one remains at Groote Schuur in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a> and a soapstone figurine is in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the Great Enclosure and its Conical Tower; the eight soapstone <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a> Birds; trade in gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> coast; Chinese and Persian imports; the colonial 'Zimbabwe controversy'; the name of the modern state</span></div>
