    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Great Zimbabwe grew rich from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and from controlling the trade in gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> between the Zimbabwean Plateau and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> coast.</li><li>Power and trade shifted north and west in the 15th and 16th centuries, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutapa-empire">Mutapa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khami">Khami</a>, and the city was abandoned in the 16th or 17th century.</li><li>Rhodesia's white government pressed archaeologists to deny that black Africans built it; its African origin became the scholarly consensus by the 1950s, and independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a> took its name and its bird.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>When the Portuguese captain Vicente Pegado recorded the ruins in 1531, he wrote that 'the natives of the country call these edifices Symbaoe, which according to their language signifies court'.</p></aside>
    </section>

